#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087456 :question/id #uuid "34c357f1-505b-530f-8bb4-376eada248a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2301" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$" :question/worked-solution "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4514e843-dc0c-5be5-9e96-42ad1bfc870e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{{v}^{2}+1})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272978} {:answer/id #uuid "680783c7-a79b-52f6-8c63-e5a4705ea03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273285} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0d77cc-d328-5023-810b-e3a1f565a8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273437} {:answer/id #uuid "b9914a60-1aeb-5bf6-9bfb-f20416011265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{3}-{v}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273999} {:answer/id #uuid "f794ae15-8082-5a6e-b5d7-76a88fb45ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{{v}^{2}+v}{3{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680783c7-a79b-52f6-8c63-e5a4705ea03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273285} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c0796e-4620-5dae-9cfd-a686aee98f07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275042}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "695b49d1-9327-503a-b244-1f7f385f59ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-14315" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{v{x}^{3}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}\\cancel{{x}^{3}}}{v\\cancel{{x}^{3}}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}-{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ (\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb6dd3b-cac4-56a1-b66d-59181029c178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351700} {:answer/id #uuid "6c07fbd9-126c-50e4-9dbf-421b28f03956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352523} {:answer/id #uuid "b595eb22-625b-5bb1-8e42-847d5c60fc39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353123} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a7e3b8-ab6a-52d1-a9b2-9dae8f6322f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353410} {:answer/id #uuid "d6351ed0-f215-5d36-b70b-cf1350e332e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fb6dd3b-cac4-56a1-b66d-59181029c178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351700} :answer-field/id #uuid "d40102e8-bbfb-507e-87af-b7854b0ffc32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354175}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6aa0e045-65b8-5bd8-8a66-10e8e133beb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8745" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{vx})+\\frac{vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a63a78-d8f8-520d-9e1a-a547cbc802ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272882} {:answer/id #uuid "5798a05b-9e8b-5365-ba0d-17eaf8a8607b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273138} {:answer/id #uuid "610c7517-5cb8-53dc-8041-977fdbde02dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273226} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd95ac9-8774-563b-bda6-a758e1302a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+2{v}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273341} {:answer/id #uuid "df13e973-3a12-5311-937b-d3dcae4d2226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}}))\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "610c7517-5cb8-53dc-8041-977fdbde02dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273226} :answer-field/id #uuid "15ca98bc-dec3-575a-b42c-45d374e930cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274674}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4e19450-4e51-548d-b019-a1f8398b116f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14314" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{v}^{2}{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+3v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03125177-b036-57f5-a72a-3f4420748f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1+v+4{v}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272411} {:answer/id #uuid "28abf496-f945-5a46-836c-05b71cb7c41f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272747} {:answer/id #uuid "6576c1eb-f6de-5994-856a-222b13c2c25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3v-4{v}^{2}}{1+{v}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273255} {:answer/id #uuid "67a7dc74-a18e-510b-960e-2d9d5a7efdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-v-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273280} {:answer/id #uuid "f3fe81de-e037-50b5-aa26-4a8e804b13c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-3v-4{v}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28abf496-f945-5a46-836c-05b71cb7c41f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272747} :answer-field/id #uuid "90700ab5-eeb4-56f3-8e94-25230b50b1ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f48980a9-f2e7-5b29-aaff-6351dbf19d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-14316" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x(vx)+(vx)^{2}}{(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(1-v+{v}^{2})}{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a81aecc-db87-5864-bf4a-a412676cae3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}-1}{1-2{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272896} {:answer/id #uuid "665d6fcb-2060-5376-afc2-497f1e6ffabe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{3}+2{v}^{2}}{1-{v}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273264} {:answer/id #uuid "7e690b1e-0568-56f8-9cca-4a717d1758a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+2v}{1-{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273470} {:answer/id #uuid "90821e35-81ef-5b74-b430-242af8a269fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{v+2}{1-3v-2{v}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273644} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67682d-4991-58c9-b814-c26b2c002615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2v-3{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+2v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e690b1e-0568-56f8-9cca-4a717d1758a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+2v}{1-{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273470} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcef863b-24d8-5be2-a251-e922d0413380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274967}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "590e41cc-f7e5-52a9-8e4b-08ef663bf420" :question/math-academy-id "q-14323" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+2y{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+2y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+x(vx)^{2}-(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+2(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}(1+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3})}{{x}^{3}({v}^{2}+2v)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+2v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+2v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}-{v}^{3}-2{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+2v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+2v} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+2v}{1-{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0824f56f-20f9-5a23-a152-8fec76baadc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}+3}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351637} {:answer/id #uuid "c63c9edf-13ba-5348-a784-f544f8f7798a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-3{v}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353287} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bb8d1f-0d25-549e-bf56-0e3e7da24b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}-5v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353298} {:answer/id #uuid "ea09a4b8-ac8e-5fa4-97cc-6fd6b8f5c6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353605} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dfbe1c-3320-559f-b09b-1366b8a1de9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9dfbe1c-3320-559f-b09b-1366b8a1de9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353752} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a68c90-9059-546d-a30a-825cc44d6aa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353961}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ed802b3-b056-5f83-8a03-3932e7ced220" :question/math-academy-id "q-8747" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{vx}{x})} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37195fe4-1e69-5e29-9f64-88c2decbd220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355158} {:answer/id #uuid "d7944865-4cd9-5653-8711-296c10bff723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356552} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb55cc2-621e-5cbe-9cd9-9ff6c96826e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356599} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb8b2a1-b08b-5a4b-bd39-b2a6e5fddc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356600} {:answer/id #uuid "e91d297a-9ec5-5912-aa6d-9d3765e8b68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{1}{v}))\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7944865-4cd9-5653-8711-296c10bff723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356552} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02fb817-012c-5fd8-afd9-ec9e45613dab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357240}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "647415db-3b65-5b2e-8b3d-f70381af49e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-90601" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}).\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{vx}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{vx}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457 :question/id #uuid "89b7276c-ef41-5123-ad31-b54753656111" :question/math-academy-id "e-2302" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](images/12/12aec388c94c8c4d4f2099b98f5a489d110fa3ef0d58e7895d9a425e49f8eb77.png)" :question/worked-solution "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n![](images/c3/c3215663fd1a332f750b8af4049404061e2b1c64726c81572d1a1e76816bb718.png)\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n![](images/7c/7c57d5a7c4003269ba9eacf6240394f508347989a9d75665c7070e31cbdad701.png)\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n![](images/8a/8ad7a31876bc67fa69ff24ed41badcf6531ddf52b2d1847af4b0b75595ab5bcd.png)\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n![](images/7a/7a6577d6ac4f3f1fc100a2767d77509fbe211f7d3e7bc7a2bba027080e7e6ccd.png)\nwhere ![](images/42/4247e7a16afb46979bc6ecfc9ae10dc6c92d0f5f1ff5e1b2a1f412ec86a48a83.png) (with ![](images/9e/9e71f72815a80396d1186bfed1047662e1d507561c7160d1c2d89c2f23421c09.png)) and ![](images/01/012dd077bd0f68f82ae37fa50d493e97bd67ac5ff9f301e48ca29e91b1af16ab.png) \n\nFinally, substituting ![](images/f7/f74fcd854fc4be51cd8eed8f4d0649bcc7cf11089a7aa2a7e7edd8d306693f78.png) and simplifying, we get\n![](images/05/05eae130ba10a3386a1c8621ecc3e3d3ec0bb7af352fb4c928cf8bf2d88c21a3.png)\nwhere ![](images/89/8939a3ee49356a0cc8515cce98d84ecf10279dad9d2f76fac07660d0f99a0d7b.png) Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/18/185e5a72b055183d70fb6d9dbc0227190a8114b80b2dc4193d8f542d5c85c0d7.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49eaf082-07de-5468-a2d7-4cdfce27a553" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697 :topic/math-academy-id 466 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Separation of Variables"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f095d9d-73ca-5510-90d9-13b6f42d391c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = Kx^{4} - 3x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087318} {:answer/id #uuid "14ce0339-1417-5b9c-b34f-15d48bba12ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = Kx^{3} - x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087319} {:answer/id #uuid "23493c53-f790-52b7-8d4b-a2a247c6a8df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = Kx^{3} + 2x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087320} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5f3ce3-6b89-5309-ab95-65da4990d433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{3} = Kx^{3} - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087321} {:answer/id #uuid "5e069116-da75-542a-b369-bd8e185c2346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = Kx^{4} + x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087322}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5a5051-ab3a-58d2-839d-37c5299136cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087317}] :question/id #uuid "ecc5b0a4-8e5a-5917-a8c3-d4ab3b2b520d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8843" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x^{2} + 3y^{2}}{2xy}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033b5955-1071-5d95-b2f7-c2bd33d3768d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C{x}^{3}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351594} {:answer/id #uuid "16da157c-25e8-539b-972d-839e07401032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-{x}^{3}+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351768} {:answer/id #uuid "44d00c81-de3d-54d8-a887-f6cce43dbe9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352200} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec6f001-1f40-5e1d-8eaf-f6d17c1f89cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+Cx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352407} {:answer/id #uuid "d8863625-e75e-5388-909d-087df892fe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8863625-e75e-5388-909d-087df892fe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353447} :answer-field/id #uuid "3026f036-9a48-51a7-b01d-a4f3f08da8e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353897}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19530196-afb6-5c10-98a9-988afc75a28c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8844" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $2$ (i.e., they are quadratic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}-{x}^{2}}{x(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}-1)}{v{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1}{v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1-{v}^{2}}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4240f0e5-b1a6-5f98-993e-7c328c878785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\frac{y}{x}=C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272953} {:answer/id #uuid "62bc8038-4d46-561e-ab4d-c5512986257f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}|+2\\frac{y}{x}=C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273236} {:answer/id #uuid "78fcca57-3793-54d9-9ed6-e1885a1fdd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}=C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273426} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed55e10-2aae-5111-b574-774642465adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}=C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273475} {:answer/id #uuid "aec77027-a6c4-54d9-8f83-8cb3685f4304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}|+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}=C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed55e10-2aae-5111-b574-774642465adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}=C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273475} :answer-field/id #uuid "729c88dd-5291-5373-98f6-d82abc2b83bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c139f739-608f-51ea-bcb4-01f8a92a8c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-14318" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{yx}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{yx}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)x}{(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{v{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{v}+\\frac{1}{{v}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{2{v}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =C \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d9fe79d-af55-52cc-807d-fdf8ff575196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273336} {:answer/id #uuid "7a705b5a-78f9-5bf4-bce8-236b06ad49a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273438} {:answer/id #uuid "83d1456f-a5d0-5b18-8867-aafba772b364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273519} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5879f0-7e3a-5185-9b2f-9e9609b66dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273742} {:answer/id #uuid "e17a8c50-895e-531b-acd7-eacf19bd14fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a705b5a-78f9-5bf4-bce8-236b06ad49a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273438} :answer-field/id #uuid "35568be0-5728-557a-8bc2-7bde66432758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274727}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30f68eaf-916e-557d-ad03-5e30a2efe0b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-14321" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $3$ (i.e., they are cubic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{3}+x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}(vx)+{x}^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}({v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1)}{{x}^{3}({v}^{2}+v)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1}{{v}^{2}+v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1}{{v}^{2}+v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1-{v}^{3}-{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v+1}{v(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31321e37-1d8f-571b-93be-de318d95581f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+{e}^{Kx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352023} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5509a4-9f52-54c9-a17c-80239e35bbab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352393} {:answer/id #uuid "91cfd736-1c68-561f-a5b9-ea0cbbe64c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=K(2+{e}^{3x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352829} {:answer/id #uuid "a325eecd-f037-58fb-8914-e5fc2b454b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{Kx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352960} {:answer/id #uuid "dff7fb2b-7bf1-5f3a-9233-5a286fcbeca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=K{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a325eecd-f037-58fb-8914-e5fc2b454b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{Kx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352960} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3b6679e-da5e-570b-8272-44a851986baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354240}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b284307a-7bac-5101-a812-75d87d197409" :question/math-academy-id "q-8845" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})+1)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{vx}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{vx}{x})+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo evaluate the integral on the left-hand side, we use integration by substitution with\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{ln}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\frac{1}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{u}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡v| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡v| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{C} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{C}|x|) \\\\ |\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =|x|{e}^{C} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x} & =\\pm {e}^{C}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x} & =Kx \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & ={e}^{Kx} \\\\ y & =x{e}^{Kx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced the new constant of integration:\n\n$$\nK=\\pm {e}^{C}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433c74fb-43f7-5727-9983-010f8c211f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{4}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355264} {:answer/id #uuid "55a4be35-898b-5439-adca-7b36f1ff4970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{C+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355425} {:answer/id #uuid "a96d1ec6-5cb0-56d5-9973-2123a3ad4d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356163} {:answer/id #uuid "cc536521-0c37-5947-8b9c-28f5c636543e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{C+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356447} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb3678b-a603-59dc-9e12-878859c35e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96d1ec6-5cb0-56d5-9973-2123a3ad4d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356163} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd025383-e1bb-5f14-a031-96c47932a3f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357310}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51ab68ae-6386-58f8-941d-fb2c589605a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-90688" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{({v}^{2}+1){x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (v+\\frac{1}{v})\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({v}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) & =C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})} & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324d9632-b193-587f-bfb5-d3128eedb441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355105} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea6a89a-49ac-5d60-8c9f-b573869dd1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355366} {:answer/id #uuid "a748de52-875c-59b6-9078-bb8480995243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356146} {:answer/id #uuid "b93929bd-c6ff-5cde-a0dc-c9ed5ef022e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356286} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a14662-7280-54dd-9109-57e225b61eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b93929bd-c6ff-5cde-a0dc-c9ed5ef022e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356286} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa93ac0-f184-5991-a79d-78c9b1ef6575" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357295}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b4ed6f3-beb2-5be5-83cb-e8d41e7b8f3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90691" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}(1+{v}^{3})}{{v}^{2}{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1+{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}}+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ {v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0230625f-08fa-547b-9a26-94bd2ec91119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354675} {:answer/id #uuid "30fd4d79-be88-5dd8-9bab-ff864e0801bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355093} {:answer/id #uuid "351f175f-a66a-537e-a899-d5e28a2bf51f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355136} {:answer/id #uuid "9738c27c-b600-5b8d-bf29-19ef94ea0e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355991} {:answer/id #uuid "ab45bf3d-adde-5478-b43f-fad1e155bfef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9738c27c-b600-5b8d-bf29-19ef94ea0e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355991} :answer-field/id #uuid "008bd79e-26b4-5414-8726-6a649de49016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356909}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed854c8a-5a47-5533-b48c-04deb7b81625" :question/math-academy-id "q-90692" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+2y}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+2(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+2v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+2v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1+v \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{C}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{C}_{1}}|x|) \\\\ |v+1| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}|x| \\\\ v+1 & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}x \\\\ v+1 & =Cx \\\\ v & =Cx-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =Cx-1 \\\\ y & =C{x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3db1daca-94f7-5fc9-a2ae-4b63f7bd12e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355215} {:answer/id #uuid "4053804e-1ea5-55b1-bd74-16ff29c5bfb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355239} {:answer/id #uuid "538b21c4-4f74-52e0-9fdc-8f28be2e423d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355407} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa45948-76de-50a2-ab36-7a90839c9027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355890} {:answer/id #uuid "a42f0e08-13ac-59b0-9899-3b551131684e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "538b21c4-4f74-52e0-9fdc-8f28be2e423d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355407} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a371f5e-aae2-5ed7-90ed-8749fc23270a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357101}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73b06f86-eb7f-5333-916f-45bb6952f791" :question/math-academy-id "q-90693" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184071 :question/id #uuid "78547385-d46f-5a2a-bfc1-39e040208ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5426" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  ![](images/a7/a7171d7bdd2b5c79b55ce6bd204e36304640468bd2ad533ac4032cd5a4ddc2ae.svg) \n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n![](images/ab/ab1cd4be22e0d3344318108914d506f9fc259446654eefff94a36d38afac3f8b.svg)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n![](images/e7/e7660dfd412f13c5d20dd9e77ec439cdbf2916ebfbccc383d13b5f9ed4032cda.svg) \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a17adadd-02fe-5b13-a556-c4b68557dec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183140} {:answer/id #uuid "f44f8717-6bc7-5c29-a85d-01286768fa78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183141} {:answer/id #uuid "3493b4eb-bc91-59c3-abe3-1362d436c2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183142} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5d43af-17a2-5f2e-916b-b2453eace3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183143} {:answer/id #uuid "62186aa1-6d7f-5a56-8e00-28c657c126cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183144}] :answer-field/id #uuid "405282dd-7ce6-56bf-8492-d4b7cbb14f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183139}] :question/id #uuid "4d185801-460b-5883-a896-8f1107477aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-34930" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix and\n\n$Null(A) = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}$?\n\n1. The number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $3$\n2. $Col(A) = R^{4}$\n3. $rank(A) = 2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148c103f-607d-5f86-b626-7f905a9009be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183152} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5566c6-f36e-5fa6-963f-ff29a059239e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183153} {:answer/id #uuid "1eee22c0-aaee-5c76-b935-89d9e34d716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183154} {:answer/id #uuid "21dd967c-ba0d-5081-815f-e4707b87b16d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183155} {:answer/id #uuid "6a680598-5a75-5f51-afed-93c0cd66c558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183156}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2c0659d5-6754-5a2c-b59d-a8d4e248b40e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183151}] :question/id #uuid "50dd3dbf-46fe-5a60-971b-ec20882fc4a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-34934" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\n1. $rank(A) = 3$\n2. $A$ is invertible\n3. $nullity(A) = 1$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2659df-70b4-5256-87fa-2a34198af876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449658} {:answer/id #uuid "77a2cd60-bf16-5a97-b3a5-f6ed36dd706c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77a2cd60-bf16-5a97-b3a5-f6ed36dd706c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449659} :answer-field/id #uuid "3592b310-da06-5773-8430-cf897b63efce" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71487a84-b41a-585b-8ff3-f6fbfac9393d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449660} {:answer/id #uuid "dc36f444-1255-5d82-87f6-8355261ee483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc36f444-1255-5d82-87f6-8355261ee483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449661} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d161315-6d73-5e59-8ca0-0d5fd8273d52" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450784} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0d44763-7df9-5816-b934-fe067cf8d583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449662} {:answer/id #uuid "3786226d-53c4-521c-afec-80c21f875609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0d44763-7df9-5816-b934-fe067cf8d583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449662} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c5e784e-a241-51f7-84ca-848b80cea623" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450785}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a4ded73-dd50-5431-9dd3-a7f4831d94c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-142544" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix which has only $3$ non-pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=7$ columns and $3$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7-3=4\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n Thus,  according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $n=7$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d0391e2-c7ef-5a32-a6e4-a1dd2f23ab04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449664} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6ce1e9-a2d9-513e-9218-2fe7d1e0a8a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d0391e2-c7ef-5a32-a6e4-a1dd2f23ab04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449664} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aed2ada-8cf1-5887-add8-5891a536ebb4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450786} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af8c890-adc0-5501-b776-d23219000bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449666} {:answer/id #uuid "78ea7642-4a88-5dd4-9c0e-b9cddd5255a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af8c890-adc0-5501-b776-d23219000bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449666} :answer-field/id #uuid "e11f78a0-08ce-50e3-88fc-e1011761342e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "634c8a5c-c46d-507d-a2df-cc1a7726c974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449668} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3e28a0-079e-52e9-9123-05a2f2341769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d3e28a0-079e-52e9-9123-05a2f2341769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449669} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e79aa7-abeb-5d53-9e24-d52f8f63b70a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450788}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5342a760-4f6b-57f7-989c-35eadd9600b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143533" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)={\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n- **III.** $det(A)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5eb0fdb-7256-54c3-9a7a-a9dca48fc612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451741} {:answer/id #uuid "81ce3ea0-81f7-5844-b779-4c3cea386177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81ce3ea0-81f7-5844-b779-4c3cea386177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451742} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac36175b-5342-5095-991f-b0d75bb31fa9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7348cde9-7949-5bff-82b0-8b60d1a6b39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451743} {:answer/id #uuid "01621a61-e00e-5295-bb23-ab9f96754ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7348cde9-7949-5bff-82b0-8b60d1a6b39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451743} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d3a068-4e65-58a9-a0c0-45461411dede" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1ec200-5f46-55a8-a21f-13735ffe254a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451745} {:answer/id #uuid "6075a26d-50e6-543d-90ef-7a1fbac5e9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6075a26d-50e6-543d-90ef-7a1fbac5e9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451746} :answer-field/id #uuid "12403c46-08ab-5006-baea-a3791d434532" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452720}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "322fb855-3d8d-59e6-ba9e-0f5421e84a86" :question/math-academy-id "q-79417" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix which has only $1$ non-pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=5$ columns and $1$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5-1=4\\ne 5.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=5$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56bbd1f-d979-5241-8dc4-a8e64037e678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451747} {:answer/id #uuid "b5789825-466e-5d22-9f09-42c97e0435a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56bbd1f-d979-5241-8dc4-a8e64037e678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451747} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc17da3-e4d0-5047-880f-6b1aaa55e9cb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e85400e8-1b39-5381-89d4-9456a7b1822c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451749} {:answer/id #uuid "a330ce1f-b4c7-5d2b-9114-283fa3e27999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a330ce1f-b4c7-5d2b-9114-283fa3e27999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451750} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fca603b-0e04-57c7-b9ac-a845c8d3054f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452722} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f7bb58-5fc7-50bf-bcf6-b8a2a3b9a52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451751} {:answer/id #uuid "927e07de-2922-5088-8e17-5260e4060177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "927e07de-2922-5088-8e17-5260e4060177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451752} :answer-field/id #uuid "6904f830-30a3-5787-9c3c-630ff0631353" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "251cb9e2-c851-5082-b166-e39206cbabf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79420" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with only  $1$ pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $A$ is not  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has just $1$ pivot column, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da20820a-7cf1-52ba-a9b8-2212f98fa16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451753} {:answer/id #uuid "7aefb362-63f4-504f-98b5-a2da25f151f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da20820a-7cf1-52ba-a9b8-2212f98fa16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451753} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c53c77e-992f-545f-ba55-2353aade2814" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9882f7d4-51ae-5c58-b561-ff060c844fd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451755} {:answer/id #uuid "2541f8cb-7e6a-5880-81b4-5ca6877fea1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2541f8cb-7e6a-5880-81b4-5ca6877fea1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451756} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6fdddfe-e700-5aea-b0ef-f2081cc85fef" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452725} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "873e0c05-9845-5591-87a1-097a96eb6fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451757} {:answer/id #uuid "5eba92ea-50a0-54c0-aba7-d88034bc70b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "873e0c05-9845-5591-87a1-097a96eb6fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451757} :answer-field/id #uuid "c26a036b-3629-5b64-969c-37e14040c238" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452726}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92ccb45d-8792-5a6a-9a10-b7498ac220cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79423" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{Null}(A)={\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n- **II.** $A$ is **not** invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=1\\ne 4,$ it follows, by the invertible matrix theorem, that the matrix $A$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=1$ then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is, in fact,  a $1$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ce3c5f0-7529-5c2c-9564-44c889eb86c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453386} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0a7954-cd05-51a7-90b9-b780bf965f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce3c5f0-7529-5c2c-9564-44c889eb86c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453386} :answer-field/id #uuid "bca78a15-c48c-5741-abab-0bf7b97a57a4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454443} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcec78e2-d6bb-56c4-ad75-975ad764b8c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453388} {:answer/id #uuid "292a5262-ac7c-5df9-a70a-6d1d92a3b30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcec78e2-d6bb-56c4-ad75-975ad764b8c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453388} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd94f480-acd9-57b8-a0ef-b55824c69c2f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dd8e82d-abc1-543c-9e38-eb7d06e6eeb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453390} {:answer/id #uuid "399ad536-9e21-50bb-b29b-6a5685869bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399ad536-9e21-50bb-b29b-6a5685869bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453391} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcdeb836-29c1-560f-b756-552781011c7f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8048be79-9af8-51c0-a0c1-5b68f549139c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139986" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $3$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the generating vectors in \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nare linearly independent, we conclude that the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=1\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4.$ Now, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9290da55-a6a3-5bb8-a9b8-2039b243ee6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453404} {:answer/id #uuid "adeaa133-0730-51b1-8b37-ab1eabd66c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adeaa133-0730-51b1-8b37-ab1eabd66c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453405} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f82a48-c4e0-5658-a295-60736c7e5166" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88d7d24f-3606-50eb-9098-a34319240958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453406} {:answer/id #uuid "535a174d-2f6e-5ab0-99bb-3ccad768cc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88d7d24f-3606-50eb-9098-a34319240958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453406} :answer-field/id #uuid "59aa5f02-f270-5643-aeca-3ee7824a7629" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99558d31-6961-52e7-b662-299510c283b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453408} {:answer/id #uuid "688a37cc-bcaf-529c-a80d-90d43dc9aa3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99558d31-6961-52e7-b662-299510c283b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453408} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f387ba8-880e-5912-8b03-6a51150d10c2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454454}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "017db163-2592-561e-a975-d762dc265a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-142542" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}$\n- **III.** $A$ is  invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=5,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =5 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 5$, according to the  invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3ba979-73eb-55d9-98be-53eb63f15310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454018} {:answer/id #uuid "42f7e70e-a33c-5389-83b8-4b66c4b40413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42f7e70e-a33c-5389-83b8-4b66c4b40413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454019} :answer-field/id #uuid "51beb1c0-c434-5ffe-be4f-d1af3a966f94" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b27c5d69-4610-55e4-befd-bab1f9f21b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454020} {:answer/id #uuid "7f38039b-9493-5449-afe0-51512728ff71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f38039b-9493-5449-afe0-51512728ff71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454021} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd2f4dc-0220-560a-b969-e0fb63f01a28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454760} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22168706-fdd0-5ec9-8357-a478a254b0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454022} {:answer/id #uuid "b3eaef39-5ed2-5d0e-8448-e5d92a445686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22168706-fdd0-5ec9-8357-a478a254b0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454022} :answer-field/id #uuid "447c453c-9c02-53db-ad8b-23d0593303f4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454761}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c7ff497-8845-5d34-a5a1-e0b10d7a1e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-79367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $A$ is not  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491ec8ca-40f3-5454-809e-b6b418901802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489686} {:answer/id #uuid "4bad21d3-8ccb-5f2e-b04d-bc4550a71c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489691} {:answer/id #uuid "83029887-1c17-5621-b865-840b4e1ed3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489813} {:answer/id #uuid "8884a0e0-0a83-5ace-92f9-fb64604eefa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489827} {:answer/id #uuid "916469af-a06a-5bf7-a441-a7fdd789fe00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8884a0e0-0a83-5ace-92f9-fb64604eefa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489827} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a6251f1-889c-581d-b906-e6b75fa33975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490134}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "128f0273-6319-516f-a4e8-3d58e084f0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-142538" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix with $2$ pivot columns?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=4$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since $A$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have that $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 2+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298c6536-dd07-5d51-8c18-8ca670439c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489607} {:answer/id #uuid "37b42792-707d-5224-838b-cff46365c833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489639} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec0644e-4274-5048-9b84-1b7e49ed7bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489741} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a192c7-d581-5aec-87a2-1e74c429a5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489985} {:answer/id #uuid "df796ca4-fa04-56be-ad9e-bc125d4ef4a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37b42792-707d-5224-838b-cff46365c833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489639} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea1ac60-40da-5758-9bc0-142c36f8f6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490155}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3a4804f-9ed9-56b0-80c9-162f94bd57af" :question/math-academy-id "q-79411" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix which has exactly $2$ non-pivot columns?\n\nI. $A$ is  invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has $n=6$ columns and $2$ of them are non-pivot ones, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6-2=4\\ne 6.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=4$,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=6$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184124 :question/id #uuid "9615d8af-b005-5449-be4b-a7487375c98f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9963" :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028 :topic/math-academy-id 1867 :topic/title "The Rank of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996 :topic/math-academy-id 1868 :topic/title "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961371f2-f71a-573d-9b98-504222de4a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183134} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9bdf09-8484-50e4-907e-eeceb6bf01c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183135} {:answer/id #uuid "a9063a2f-983f-511b-8320-eb5479f9dbd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183136} {:answer/id #uuid "37b465af-8d04-53f5-a8eb-c31f56a99ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183137} {:answer/id #uuid "781325c3-e9c6-5691-9247-c29a5c79d34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9063a2f-983f-511b-8320-eb5479f9dbd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183136} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef2d522-f80e-5da1-ba53-1027339ce406" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183133}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a05d1874-f484-5b03-a7ba-b2697b84a74b" :question/math-academy-id "q-34833" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 8$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=3,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =8 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =8-3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5bc6ca8-9ac0-5308-a6b2-87c27295178c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183146} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5cc6e5-5ce0-553b-97f4-aa903d5a2873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183147} {:answer/id #uuid "06d1858b-b3ba-5fd6-be5b-352c28e50337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183148} {:answer/id #uuid "351bf408-a43f-531a-94a0-4268e98ac1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183149} {:answer/id #uuid "77c26f94-f1e0-5b92-90d4-af981ae4d341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb5cc6e5-5ce0-553b-97f4-aa903d5a2873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183147} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce549fcc-13ed-50dd-8870-5c2475c33016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183145}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "245a8171-aeb5-5caf-950d-44cbb3c0aece" :question/math-academy-id "q-34931" :question/problem "Consider a $6\\times 7$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if 